---
/**
 * games/texas-holdem.astro — mathematical framework for No-Limit Texas Hold'em.
 *
 * This is intentionally not a claim that every poker room has the same rake,
 * nor that every player has the same equity. Card-combination figures are exact;
 * decisions and player results require stated ranges, opponents and fee rules.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Texas Hold'em Poker — The Math"
  description="The mathematics of Texas Hold'em: exact card probabilities, equity, pot odds, rake, tournament fees and why poker has no universal house edge."
>
  <SiteHeader alwaysVisible />

  <main>
    <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Games</a> / <span>Cards</span> / <strong>Texas Hold'em</strong>
    </nav>

    <div class="game-header">
      <div>
        <h1>Texas Hold'em Poker</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">You play against other players; the operator's price is rake or entry fees, not a universal house edge.</p>
        <div class="header-tags">
          <span class="tag">cards</span>
          <span class="tag">community cards</span>
          <span class="tag">player vs player</span>
          <span class="tag">rake-dependent</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <dl class="summary-panel">
        <div><dt>Universal house edge</dt><dd>N/A</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Operator cost</dt><dd class="bad">Rake / fee</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Variance</dt><dd>High</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Player decisions</dt><dd class="good">Extensive</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <div class="callout key">
        <span class="callout-title">The essential distinction</span>
        <p>
          Texas Hold'em is not a player-versus-casino game like baccarat. Chips are transferred among
          players, while the room removes a published <strong>rake</strong> from eligible cash-game pots
          or an <strong>entry fee</strong> from tournament buy-ins. An individual can be profitable only
          by making better decisions than opponents by more than their share of those costs.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="how-it-works">How the game works</h2>
      <p>
        In No-Limit Texas Hold'em, each player receives two private <strong>hole cards</strong>. Five
        shared <strong>community cards</strong> are dealt in stages. A player can win by making every
        opponent fold, or at showdown by making the best five-card poker hand from any five of their
        two hole cards and the five board cards.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Pre-flop:</strong> blinds (and sometimes antes) are posted, two hole cards are dealt, then players bet.</li>
        <li><strong>Flop:</strong> three community cards are dealt, followed by a betting round.</li>
        <li><strong>Turn:</strong> a fourth community card and another betting round.</li>
        <li><strong>River:</strong> a fifth community card and the final betting round.</li>
        <li><strong>Showdown:</strong> remaining players compare their best five-card hands; the pot is awarded.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Available decisions are check, bet, call, fold and raise. “No-Limit” means a legal raise can be
        as large as the player's remaining stack, subject to the betting rules. Tied best hands
        <strong>split the pot</strong>; if an indivisible chip remains, the room's published odd-chip rule
        decides who receives it.
      </p>

      <h3>Scope of this page</h3>
      <p>
        Card probabilities below use one standard 52-card deck with no jokers. The decision examples
        use heads-up, no-further-action all-ins so their EV is transparent. They are not a complete
        strategy chart for a live or online table.
      </p>
      <div class="callout warn">
        <span class="callout-title">No single “Texas Hold'em RTP” exists</span>
        <p>
          A win probability needs known cards, board, number of opponents and opponent ranges. A cash-game
          cost needs the room's rake percentage, cap, minimum pot, no-flop-no-drop rule, fixed drops and
          jackpot fees. A tournament calculation additionally needs its fee and payout structure. Do not
          apply a number from this page to a room with different rules.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="starting-hands">Exact starting-hand mathematics</h2>
      <p>
        Before any cards are known, an unordered two-card starting hand has the following equally likely
        sample space:
      </p>
      <div class="formula">
        Total hole-card combinations = C(52, 2) = 52 × 51 ÷ 2
        <br />= <span class="result">1,326</span>
      </div>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Starting-hand category</th><th style="text-align:right">Combinations</th><th style="text-align:right">Exact probability</th><th>Derivation</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Any pocket pair</td><td class="num">78</td><td class="num">5.8824%</td><td>13 ranks × C(4, 2)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A specific pair, e.g. A-A</td><td class="num">6</td><td class="num">0.4525%</td><td>C(4, 2)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Suited, non-pair</td><td class="num">312</td><td class="num">23.5294%</td><td>4 suits × C(13, 2)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Offsuit, non-pair</td><td class="num">936</td><td class="num">70.5882%</td><td>C(13, 2) × 12</td></tr>
          <tr class="highlight"><td>Total</td><td class="num">1,326</td><td class="num">100%</td><td>All mutually exclusive hole-card combinations</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        For A-A specifically, there are six favourable pairs of aces among 1,326 possible two-card hands:
      </p>
      <div class="formula">
        P(A-A) = C(4, 2) / C(52, 2) = 6 / 1,326
        <br />= <span class="result">0.0045249… = 0.4525%</span> (about 1 in 221)
      </div>
      <p>
        These are deal probabilities, not winning probabilities. For example, A-A's chance to win changes
        sharply with the number of opponents, their ranges, the board and whether they can fold.
      </p>

      <h2 id="seven-card-hands">Best five-card hand after seven cards</h2>
      <p>
        At showdown a player has access to seven cards: two hole cards plus five community cards. If those
        seven cards are treated as a uniformly random seven-card set, there are <code>C(52, 7) = 133,784,560</code>
        sets. The table classifies the <em>best</em> five-card hand in each set, so categories do not overlap.
        It describes hand frequency only—not equity against an opponent.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Best hand</th><th style="text-align:right">Seven-card sets</th><th style="text-align:right">Probability</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>High card</td><td class="num">23,294,460</td><td class="num">17.4119%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>One pair</td><td class="num">58,627,800</td><td class="num">43.8225%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Two pair</td><td class="num">31,433,400</td><td class="num">23.4955%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Three of a kind</td><td class="num">6,461,620</td><td class="num">4.8299%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Straight</td><td class="num">6,180,020</td><td class="num">4.6194%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Flush</td><td class="num">4,047,644</td><td class="num">3.0255%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Full house</td><td class="num">3,473,184</td><td class="num">2.5961%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Four of a kind</td><td class="num">224,848</td><td class="num">0.1681%</td></tr>
          <tr class="highlight"><td>Straight flush, including royal flush</td><td class="num">41,584</td><td class="num">0.0311%</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>133,784,560</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>100%</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2 id="equity-and-pot-odds">Equity, pot odds and a call's EV</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Equity</strong> is a player's expected share of the final pot: a win counts as 1, a loss as
        0 and a split as its fraction of the pot. It is conditional on the opponent range and all known
        cards. In a no-more-betting all-in, let <code>q</code> be your equity, <code>P</code> the pot before
        your call, <code>C</code> the call and <code>R</code> the rake charged from the final pot. The
        incremental value of calling rather than folding is:
      </p>
      <div class="formula">
        EV(call) = q × (P + C − R) − C
        <br />Required equity = C / (P + C − R)
      </div>
      <p>
        Example: the pot is $150 before your $50 call. The gross final pot is $200. Under an explicitly
        hypothetical 5% rake capped at $10, <code>R = min(0.05 × 200, 10) = $10</code>, so the winner
        receives $190:
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Equity</th><th style="text-align:right">Calculation</th><th style="text-align:right">EV of calling</th><th>Decision in this simplified spot</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>25%</td><td class="num">0.25 × $190 − $50</td><td class="num">−$2.50</td><td>Fold</td></tr>
          <tr class="highlight"><td>30%</td><td class="num">0.30 × $190 − $50</td><td class="num">+$7.00</td><td>Call</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="formula">
        Required equity = $50 / $190 = <span class="result">26.32%</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        Without rake, the threshold would be 25%. Rake therefore turns some marginally profitable calls
        into folds. In a real hand, future betting, the possibility of making an opponent fold, implied
        odds, reverse implied odds and range errors also matter; simple pot odds alone do not solve every
        decision.
      </p>

      <h2 id="rake">Cash-game rake: the operator's mathematical edge</h2>
      <p>
        A cash-game room commonly takes a percentage of eligible pots, up to a cap. A transparent example
        of a <em>hypothetical</em> schedule is:</n      </p>
      <div class="formula">
        R(P) = min(0.05 × P, $10), charged only to a contested flop pot
      </div>
      <p>
        Here <code>P</code> is the gross pot and <code>R(P)</code> is the rake. This is not asserted to be
        any particular room's schedule. Real schedules can have different percentages and caps, a minimum
        pot before rake, no-flop-no-drop, fixed time charges, jackpot drops or separate promotional fees.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Gross eligible pot</th><th style="text-align:right">Rake under this example</th><th style="text-align:right">Winner receives</th><th style="text-align:right">Effective rake</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>$20</td><td class="num">$1.00</td><td class="num">$19.00</td><td class="num">5.00%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>$100</td><td class="num">$5.00</td><td class="num">$95.00</td><td class="num">5.00%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>$200</td><td class="num">$10.00</td><td class="num">$190.00</td><td class="num">5.00%</td></tr>
          <tr class="highlight"><td>$500</td><td class="num">$10.00</td><td class="num">$490.00</td><td class="num">2.00%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Before costs, poker is zero-sum across the players. After the rake is removed, the table-wide
        expectation is negative by exactly that removal:
      </p>
      <div class="formula">
        Σ EV(players in a hand) = −E[R(P) + other posted fees]
      </div>
      <p>
        This equation does <strong>not</strong> allocate the same cost to every player or prove that a
        particular player loses. A player with a sufficient skill edge can have positive EV; a player who
        merely breaks even against opponents before costs loses after their effective rake burden.
      </p>

      <h2 id="tournaments">Tournament fees are different from per-pot rake</h2>
      <p>
        For a tournament advertised as <strong>$100 + $9</strong>, $100 per entry goes to the prize pool and
        $9 is the operator fee. With <code>N</code> equally skilled entrants, the prize pool is <code>100N</code>,
        so each entrant's expected prize before skill differences is <code>100N / N = $100</code>.
      </p>
      <div class="formula">
        EV for an equal-skill entrant = expected prize − total entry
        <br />= $100 − ($100 + $9) = <span class="result">−$9</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        That is a 9% fee relative to the $100 prize-pool buy-in, or 8.26% of the $109 total entry. An
        individual player's tournament EV can be positive only if their expected share of the prize pool
        exceeds $109. Payout structure and survival incentives mean that chip EV is not automatically the
        same as cash EV; tournament analysis often needs an ICM or another explicit payout model.
      </p>

      <h2 id="strategy">Strategy: what the mathematics supports</h2>
      <p>
        Unlike a fixed-rule casino game, Hold'em has no short universal strategy that is correct without
        position, stack depth, prior action, table size, opponent ranges and rake. Mathematics supports
        comparing specific actions under stated assumptions.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What it measures</th><th>What it does not guarantee</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Position</td><td>How much information is available before acting</td><td>That every late-position hand is profitable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Range vs range equity</td><td>Expected share of a pot given specified hand distributions</td><td>Exact equity if the ranges are wrong</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Pot odds</td><td>Minimum equity in a static no-more-action pot</td><td>Future betting, fold equity or rake-free profitability</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fold equity</td><td>Value created when an opponent folds to a bet or raise</td><td>That a bluff works often enough without a range model</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bankroll management</td><td>Risk of ruin and size of swings</td><td>Positive EV from losing decisions</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="callout warn">
        <span class="callout-title">No progression beats rake</span>
        <p>
          Increasing a stake after losses can change variance and risk of ruin, but it cannot repair a
          negative decision. If a sequence of decisions has expected values <code>EV₁, EV₂, …, EVₙ</code>,
          then the expected total is <code>Σ EVᵢ</code>; changing the order or using a Martingale does not
          make the rake disappear.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="side-bets">Side bets, insurance and promotions</h2>
      <p>
        Standard Texas Hold'em has no universal casino side bet. Some rooms offer jackpot drops, bomb pots,
        insurance, time charges or local promotional awards. They are separate products: their rules,
        qualification conditions, fees and payouts must be published before an EV can be calculated.
      </p>
      <p>
        A “bonus” cannot be labelled good value from its name alone. For any optional wager with outcomes
        <code>i</code>, probabilities <code>pᵢ</code> and net payouts <code>xᵢ</code>, its value is:</n      </p>
      <div class="formula">
        EV(optional wager) = Σ(p<sub>i</sub> × x<sub>i</sub>)
      </div>
      <p>
        Do not use an ante-bonus or insurance percentage from another venue: a changed qualifying hand,
        payout or fee changes the EV. This page intentionally does not invent a generic side-bet house edge.
      </p>

      <h2 id="methodology">Methodology and limits</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Starting-hand figures are exact combinations from <code>C(52, 2)</code>.</li>
        <li>Seven-card hand frequencies are exact counts from <code>C(52, 7)</code>; they are not opponent equity.</li>
        <li>The pot-odds calculation is exact for its stated, no-further-action example and stated hypothetical rake.</li>
        <li>No Monte Carlo or GTO simulation is claimed on this page. A useful simulator would need disclosed ranges, betting policy, fee schedule, seed and validation target.</li>
        <li>Cash-game and tournament results are model-dependent. Always check the room's current written rules and fee schedule.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="sources">Sources &amp; methodology</h2>
      <ul class="sources">
        <li><a href="https://www.wsop.com/poker-games/texas-holdem/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">World Series of Poker — Texas Hold'em</a>: game sequence and hand-ranking reference.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.pokerstars.com/poker/learn/how-to-play/texas-holdem-rules/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PokerStars Learn — How to play Texas Hold'em</a>: rules and betting-round reference.</li>
        <li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poker_probability" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Poker probability</a>: independent reference for seven-card hand-category frequencies; counts on this page are shown explicitly.</li>
        <li>Rake and tournament examples on this page are illustrative formulas, not a claim about a particular poker room. The primary source for an actual calculation must be that room's published rake or fee schedule.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="edit-link">
        <p>Spot an error or want to improve this analysis? <a href="https://github.com/Suste69/OpenFiches">Open an issue or contribute on GitHub →</a></p>
        <a href="https://github.com/Suste69/OpenFiches/edit/main/src/pages/games/texas-holdem.astro">Edit this page on GitHub →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
